<header class="ref-head"><span class="hero-ornament"></span><h1 class="research-title">21 January in history</h1><p class="ref-lede">8 events that happened on 21 January, from 1326 to 1976.</p></header>
<ol class="day-events"><li class="tih-event-card tih-has-thumb tih-event-hero"><p class="tih-hero-kicker">&#10022; Anniversary of the day</p><a class="tih-event-thumb" href="/encyclopedia/oxford" tabindex="-1" aria-hidden="true"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/oxford.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="168" height="112"></a><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/years/1326">1326 CE</a> <span class="tih-ago">700 years ago</span></p><h2 class="tih-event-title">Edward II issues the foundation charter of Oriel College, Oxford</h2><p class="tih-event-desc">King Edward II issued a charter founding Oriel College, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oxford">Oxford</a>, in his own name, on properties handed to him by his almoner, Adam de Brome. De Brome became its first Provost, and the college was re-endowed with Tackley's Inn and Perilous Hall.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edward-ii-of-england">Edward II of England</a></div></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/years/1525">1525 CE</a> <span class="tih-ago">501 years ago</span></p><h2 class="tih-event-title">First adult baptisms in Zurich launch the Anabaptist movement</h2><p class="tih-event-desc">Conrad Grebel, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/felix-manz">Felix Manz</a> and George Blaurock baptised one another as believing adults in Zurich. Fiercely persecuted, their movement gave rise to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mennonites">Mennonites</a>, Amish and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hutterites">Hutterites</a>.</p></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/years/1793">1793 CE</a> <span class="tih-ago">233 years ago</span></p><h2 class="tih-event-title">Louis XVI is guillotined in Paris</h2><p class="tih-event-desc">Convicted of conspiracy against the nation, the former king was executed in the Place de la Révolution. His death set revolutionary <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> against the crowned heads of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a></div></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/years/1861">1861 CE</a> <span class="tih-ago">165 years ago</span></p><h2 class="tih-event-title">Jefferson Davis bids farewell to the US Senate</h2><p class="tih-event-desc">Following <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mississippi-river">Mississippi</a>'s secession, Davis and four other Southern senators gave farewell speeches and resigned. Within weeks he was chosen as President of the Confederate States.</p></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/years/1908">1908 CE</a> <span class="tih-ago">118 years ago</span></p><h2 class="tih-event-title">New York passes an ordinance banning women from smoking in public</h2><p class="tih-event-desc">The Sullivan Ordinance barred managers of public places from allowing women to smoke. Mayor George McClellan vetoed it two weeks later, but it reflected anxieties about women's changing roles.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-york">New York</a></div></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/years/1924">1924 CE</a> <span class="tih-ago">102 years ago</span></p><h2 class="tih-event-title">Vladimir Lenin dies at Gorki</h2><p class="tih-event-desc">The founder of the Soviet state died aged 53 after a series of strokes. His body was embalmed and displayed in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/red-square">Red Square</a>, and Stalin outmanoeuvred Trotsky and others to succeed him.</p></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/years/1954">1954 CE</a> <span class="tih-ago">72 years ago</span></p><h2 class="tih-event-title">USS Nautilus, the first nuclear-powered submarine, is launched</h2><p class="tih-event-desc">First Lady Mamie <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dwight-d-eisenhower">Eisenhower</a> christened Nautilus at Groton, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/connecticut">Connecticut</a>. Able to stay submerged for weeks, it sailed under the North Pole in 1958 and transformed undersea warfare.</p></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/years/1976">1976 CE</a> <span class="tih-ago">50 years ago</span></p><h2 class="tih-event-title">Concorde begins commercial supersonic service</h2><p class="tih-event-desc">British Airways flew Concorde from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bahrain">Bahrain</a> and Air <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rio-de-janeiro">Rio de Janeiro</a> via <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dakar-senegal">Dakar</a>. Flying at twice the speed of sound, Concorde served passengers until 2003.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rio-de-janeiro">Rio de Janeiro</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bahrain">Bahrain</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a></div></div></li></ol>
